Recorded playback browser ownership and failure cleanup
========================================================

RecordedHlsPlayer owns its session for one camera/time/quality selection. It
releases that session once on unmount, selection change or terminal playback
failure, even when the parent leaves the failed component visible. Release
clears heartbeats and startup timers and aborts its pending manifest request
and polling delay. A start response arriving after unmount is stopped without
starting media polling. Late errors from an old player cannot affect its
replacement session.

The 30-second manifest deadline is independent of fetch and response-body
completion, so a stalled network request does not bypass it. Not-ready404
manifests and transient network errors can retry until that deadline. Successful
playback, including a deliberately paused player, keeps its regular heartbeat.
A transient heartbeat503 does not alone terminate working playback.

A definitive heartbeat401/403/404 stops local activity and attempts cleanup.
Start/manifest/heartbeat401/403 display failure without snapshot fallback;
authority refusal is not repaired by changing transport. Heartbeat404 may use
the existing unavailable callback to select another playback path. Existing
recorded-source422 (undecodable, no fallback) and501 (unsupported, fallback)
semantics remain. Demo mode still makes no real HLS start or manifest requests.

Stop is best effort: a disconnected client or revoked permission may prevent
its stop request from reaching/succeeding at the server. Server idle expiry
remains necessary. This browser lifecycle fix is not a server-side viewer lease,
aggregate quota, vendor interoperability guarantee or seamless owner failover.
Component tests exercise actual React effects/timers with explicit API/media
fixtures; packaged runtime/browser and hardware qualification remain separate.
